# Automated Test System

This file tracks the eight staged steps of building an automated unit/integration test
system for WhenWorksWeb, kept together in one file rather than split across
`Spec/Features/` because they're staged parts of a single initiative — GitHub epic #54 —
rather than independent items (see CLAUDE.md's Specs section for when grouped entries like
this are appropriate vs. one file per item). This file is scoped to these eight steps only;

No test project exists in the repo today. Controllers hold nearly all business logic (by
design, per `CODING_CONVENTIONS.md`), so the test system is built around a three-tier
architecture rather than one uniform style:

- **Tier 1** — pure unit tests for `Services/` and `Models/`/`Common` validation logic, no
  database involved.
- **Tier 2** — controller tests against a real EF Core store backed by SQLite `:memory:`
  (not the EF InMemory provider, which silently ignores relational/constraint bugs a real
  database would catch). This is the primary coverage tier, since controllers are where the
  logic lives.
- **Tier 3** — a deliberately small set of `WebApplicationFactory<Program>` full-pipeline
  smoke tests, for the handful of behaviors (routing, auth middleware, anti-forgery filters)
  that only the real HTTP pipeline exercises.

Cross-cutting decisions that apply to every step below: test data is built via hand-rolled
builder/object-mother classes per entity, not AutoFixture/Bogus; assertions are plain xUnit
`Assert`, not FluentAssertions (commercially licensed as of v8) or Shouldly; mocking, if ever
needed, uses NSubstitute rather than Moq (which had a 2023 supply-chain/telemetry incident);
test files mirror the main project's folder structure (`Controllers/`, `Services/`,
`Models/`) so a source file has a predictable corresponding test file path.

The steps are staged in dependency order: Step 1 blocks every other step. Step 2 should land
before Step 8 (the hook in Step 8 depends on the naming/path convention Step 2 documents).
Step 4 blocks Steps 5 and 7 (both need the SQLite harness). Step 6 blocks Step 7's
authorization/anti-forgery tests (those require the real HTTP pipeline, not the SQLite-only
harness). A CI (GitHub Actions) test gate is explicitly out of scope for all eight steps —
that's deferred to a future, separate decision, per epic #54's Notes.

---

## Step 1: Scaffold WhenWorksWeb.Tests project

### Status
Implemented.

### GitHub Issue
#55

### Summary
Create the `WhenWorksWeb.Tests` xUnit project and wire it into the solution so every later
step has a place to add tests.

### Motivation
No test project exists yet. Every other step in this initiative needs somewhere to live, so
this is the foundational step: get an empty, correctly-wired project building and running
before any test logic is added.

### Proposed Changes
- New xUnit project `WhenWorksWeb.Tests`, created via `dotnet new xunit`, added to the
  solution file.
- Project reference from `WhenWorksWeb.Tests` to `WhenWorksWeb` so `Controllers/`,
  `Services/`, `Models/`, and `Data/` namespaces are accessible.
- `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing` package added now (used later by Step 6's
  `WebApplicationFactory` harness, but the reference belongs with initial scaffolding).
- Folder structure inside `WhenWorksWeb.Tests` mirrors the main project (`Controllers/`,
  `Services/`, `Models/`) so later steps have an established convention to follow.

### Acceptance Criteria
- `dotnet test` runs successfully against `WhenWorksWeb.Tests` with zero tests, confirming
  the project builds and is correctly wired into the solution.
- `dotnet build` at the solution level succeeds with the new project included.

### Out of Scope
- Any actual test content (Steps 3, 5, 6, 7).
- The SQLite/WebApplicationFactory harnesses themselves (Steps 4 and 6).

---

## Step 2: Update CODING_CONVENTIONS.md with testing rules and enforcement policy

### Status
Implemented.

### GitHub Issue
#56

### Summary
Document the tiered test architecture, tooling choices, and the enforcement rule that
behavior changes must ship with corresponding test updates.

### Motivation
The testing approach for this initiative was agreed in planning conversation but isn't
written down anywhere binding yet. Codifying it in `CODING_CONVENTIONS.md` makes it the
standard future work is measured against, instead of something re-explained each time.

### Proposed Changes
- Document the three-tier test architecture (see intro above) and when each tier applies.
- State SQLite `:memory:` as the required approach for controller/EF Core tests, and rule
  out the EF InMemory provider for that purpose.
- Document the test data convention (hand-rolled builders, no AutoFixture/Bogus), the
  assertion convention (plain xUnit `Assert` only), and the mocking convention (avoid Moq;
  NSubstitute is the approved fallback).
- Add the enforcement rule: any behavior change to `Controllers/`, `Services/`, or `Models/`
  must include added/updated tests in the same change, located via the mirrored-path
  convention.
- Cross-reference (not duplicate) the existing test naming convention already documented in
  the Testing Conventions section.

### Acceptance Criteria
- `CODING_CONVENTIONS.md`'s Testing Conventions section fully describes the tiered
  architecture, tooling choices, and enforcement rule without contradicting the existing
  naming-convention text.
- No other section of `CODING_CONVENTIONS.md` is altered.

### Out of Scope
- Any actual test code or harness (Steps 1, 3-8).
- The PostToolUse hook implementation itself (Step 8) — this step only documents the
  convention the hook relies on.

