## Step 3: Tier 1 unit tests for existing Services and Models

### Status
Implemented.

### GitHub Issue
#57

### Summary
Add pure, DB-free unit tests for the app's existing `Services/` and validation logic in
`Models/`/`Common`.

### Motivation
`Services/` and `Common/ModelConstants` contain the small amount of pure logic in the app.
These are the cheapest tests to write and maintain and should exist before the heavier
controller tier is built out.

### Proposed Changes
- `Services/UniqueCodeGenerator.cs` — `GenerateUniqueCodeAsync` and `MaxAttempts` changed from
  `private` to `internal`, plus `[InternalsVisibleTo("WhenWorksWeb.Tests")]` on
  `WhenWorksWeb.csproj`. This gives tests a deterministic seam onto the retry/max-attempts loop
  via a stub existence-check delegate, independent of the actual (non-seedable, cryptographic)
  RNG behind code generation — needed to test collision-retry and exhaustion behavior at all,
  since a real collision can't be forced from outside.
- `WhenWorksWeb.Tests/Services/UniqueCodeGeneratorTests.cs` — code length/alphabet correctness,
  retry-until-success, exact-attempt-count exhaustion (pinned to `UniqueCodeGenerator.MaxAttempts`
  rather than a duplicated magic number), exception propagation from `existsAsync` (not
  swallowed/wrapped), and an RNG sanity check (many independent generations aren't all identical).
- `WhenWorksWeb.Tests/Common/ModelConstantsTests.cs` — narrowed to facts about the constants
  themselves (alphabet excludes ambiguous characters, no duplicate characters, alphabet/pattern
  character-class coherence, default color/emoji length bounds). Does not re-test validation
  behavior — see below for why that moved to real model tests.
- `WhenWorksWeb.Tests/Models/IndexViewModelTests.cs` and `ParticipantTests.cs` (plus
  `EventMessageTests.cs`/`ApplicationUserTests.cs` for parity) — test `EventCode`/`Color`
  validation via `System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations.Validator.TryValidateProperty` against
  the real model classes, exercising the actual `[Required]`/`[StringLength]`/
  `[RegularExpression]` attributes exactly as ASP.NET Core model binding would, rather than
  reimplementing an attribute's match semantics by hand in test code (which was the initial
  approach, then replaced — see below).
- **Real gap found and fixed during this step**: `Participant.Color`, `EventMessage.SenderColor`,
  and `ApplicationUser.Color` each pair `[RegularExpression(HexColorPattern)]` with
  `[StringLength(HexColorLength)]` but no `MinimumLength` and no `[Required]`. Since
  `RegularExpressionAttribute` treats null/empty as valid by design (deferring emptiness
  enforcement to `[Required]`/`MinimumLength`), an empty string passed both attributes —
  discovered when a model-level test (not the earlier pattern-only test) caught it. Not
  reachable via any live code path today (the sole entry point, `EventSignInViewModel.Color`,
  already has `[Required]`), but a latent gap for any future direct write to these properties.
  Fixed by adding `MinimumLength = HexColorLength` to all three `[StringLength]` attributes. See
  `CODING_CONVENTIONS.md`'s Domain Conventions & Gotchas for the documented version.

### Acceptance Criteria
- All Tier 1 tests run with no database, HTTP context, or controller involved.
- `dotnet test` passes for the new test files (62 tests as of this step).
- DataAnnotations-validated properties are tested via `Validator.TryValidateProperty` against the
  real model class, not a hand-rolled reimplementation of attribute match semantics.

### Out of Scope
- Any controller or HTTP-pipeline test (Steps 5-7).

### Dependencies
Step 1 (test project must exist).

---

## Step 4: SQLite in-memory test harness/fixture for controller tests

### Status
Implemented.

### GitHub Issue
#58

### Summary
Build the shared fixture that gives controller tests a real, isolated `ApplicationDbContext`
backed by SQLite `:memory:`, plus entity builder classes for test data.

### Motivation
Every controller test needs a real `ApplicationDbContext`. Without a shared fixture, each
test file would hand-roll its own connection/context setup, which doesn't scale. This is the
harness Steps 5 and 7 build on.

### Proposed Changes
- `WhenWorksWeb.Tests/Fixtures/SqliteDbContextFixture.cs` — an abstract base class that opens a
  SQLite `:memory:` connection, creates the schema via `EnsureCreated`, and exposes a fresh,
  isolated `protected ApplicationDbContext Db`. Controller test classes inherit from it; since
  xUnit constructs a new test class instance per test method, every test method gets its own
  connection and schema automatically, with no explicit reset step.
- **Real blocker found and fixed**: `ApplicationDbContext.OnModelCreating` pins SQL Server
  collation names (`SQL_Latin1_General_CP1_CI_AS`/`_CS_AS`) on `Event.Code`,
  `Participant.DisplayName`, and `Participant.RejoinCode`. SQLite has no built-in collation with
  those names, so `EnsureCreated()` failed outright with `SqliteException: no such collation
  sequence` — verified experimentally before writing the fixture. Fixed (in the fixture, not
  production code) by registering matching named collations via
  `SqliteConnection.CreateCollation`, mapping each SQL Server collation name to the equivalent
  .NET string comparison (`OrdinalIgnoreCase`/`Ordinal`). This is `Microsoft.Data.Sqlite`'s
  documented mechanism for this exact cross-provider scenario, not a workaround — verified it
  actually enforces case-insensitive uniqueness correctly (not just silences the schema-creation
  error) via a duplicate-code-different-case test. See `CODING_CONVENTIONS.md`'s "Collation
  differs by field on purpose."
- **Verified, not assumed**: SQLite's default foreign-key enforcement is OFF, unlike SQL Server.
  Confirmed EF Core's Sqlite provider enables `PRAGMA foreign_keys` automatically even for a
  pre-opened external connection (checked the pragma value directly, and confirmed a dangling-FK
  insert throws `DbUpdateException`) — no manual pragma needed in the fixture.
- `WhenWorksWeb.Tests/TestData/EventBuilder.cs`, `ParticipantBuilder.cs`, `EventDateBuilder.cs`,
  `EventMessageBuilder.cs` — hand-rolled fluent builders with schema-valid defaults for the four
  core entities, so a test only overrides the field it cares about.
- `WhenWorksWeb.Tests/Fixtures/SqliteDbContextFixtureTests.cs` — proves the fixture actually
  enforces the same relational behavior production would: builders save successfully, the
  case-insensitive unique index on `Event.Code` rejects a same-code-different-case duplicate, the
  `DisplayName` trim check constraint rejects an untrimmed value, a dangling foreign key is
  rejected, and two test methods inserting the same unique code each pass independently
  (proving per-test isolation, not just asserted).

### Acceptance Criteria
- A sample test using the fixture passes and demonstrably isolates state from other tests
  (no shared-state leakage across test runs).
- Builder classes produce valid entities satisfying all required fields/constraints.
- The fixture enforces unique indexes (including collation-dependent case-insensitivity), check
  constraints, and foreign key constraints exactly as production would — each verified by a
  dedicated test, not assumed from EnsureCreated succeeding.

### Out of Scope
- The `WebApplicationFactory` harness (Step 6) — that's a separate, full-pipeline fixture.
- Actual controller test coverage (Step 5) — this step is the harness only.

### Dependencies
Step 1. Blocks Steps 5 and 7.

---

## Step 5: Tier 2 controller test coverage for EventsController, HomeController, MyEventsController

### Status
Implemented.

### GitHub Issue
#59

### Summary
Cover the existing controller surface — `EventsController` and its partials, `HomeController`,
`MyEventsController` — using the harness from Step 4.

### Motivation
Controllers hold nearly all business logic in this app, so this is the highest-leverage
coverage in the initiative.

### Proposed Changes
- **Controller test infrastructure** (`WhenWorksWeb.Tests/Fixtures/`): `ControllerTestContext`
  (real `DefaultHttpContext`/`ControllerContext` wiring — `ClaimsPrincipal`, request cookies,
  `RequestServices` from `AddControllersWithViews()` so `TryValidateModel`/`TempData` work, and a
  minimal `StubUrlHelper` for `Url.RouteUrl`/`Url.Action` since real route resolution needs a full
  endpoint pipeline); `TestUserManagerFactory` (a real `UserManager<ApplicationUser>` resolved
  from a small DI container using `AddIdentityCore().AddEntityFrameworkStores<ApplicationDbContext>()`
  — not `new UserStore<ApplicationUser>(db)` directly, which fails to compile because
  `ApplicationUser`'s `required` members can't satisfy `UserStore<TUser>`'s `new()` constraint);
  `EventsControllerTestFixture` (shared base wiring a real `EventsController` with a single
  `EphemeralDataProtectionProvider` instance reused across every `CreateController()` call in a
  test, so a cookie protected during a simulated SignIn request can be unprotected by a later
  simulated Home request in the same test).
- `WhenWorksWeb.Tests/TestData/ApplicationUserBuilder.cs` — added for parity with the other entity
  builders, needed once controller tests required real signed-in users.
- `Controllers/HomeControllerTests.cs` — Index/Privacy/Error. (The spec originally described this
  file as covering "create/join-by-code entry points," but those actions are actually on
  `EventsController`, not `HomeController` — `HomeController` only serves Index/Privacy/Error.)
- `Controllers/EventsControllerTests.cs` (core: Create/Join),
  `Controllers/EventsControllerSignInTests.cs` (GET/POST SignIn — new participant creation,
  duplicate display name/color, missing/incorrect/correct rejoin code, conflicting account
  owner), `Controllers/EventsControllerHomeTests.cs` (Home action plus the shared access-cookie
  flow — including a full real sign-in-then-home round trip through actual cookie
  protect/unprotect, and a tampered-cookie case proving the cookie gets deleted rather than
  crashing the request) — mirroring the controller's own partial-class file split.
- `Controllers/MyEventsControllerTests.cs` — Index (challenge when unauthenticated, lists only
  created/joined events), Delete for both "event" (creator-only, Forbid otherwise) and
  "participant" modes, including a test that a participant's chat messages get their
  `ParticipantId` nulled rather than orphaned/rejected — the real FK-constraint behavior a SQLite
  backend (not EF Core InMemory) is what makes this test meaningful.

### Acceptance Criteria
- Every public action on the three controllers has at least one success-path and one
  failure-path test, where a failure path exists.
- `dotnet test` passes for all new controller tests (99 tests total as of this step).

### Out of Scope
- Authorization/anti-forgery-specific coverage (Step 7) — this step covers business-logic
  correctness, not security boundaries.
- Any Tier 3 full-pipeline test (Step 6).

### Dependencies
Step 4 (SQLite harness and entity builders).

